arguments.report:
## Bug & fix

**File changed:** `/luxon/src/impl/util.js`, function `objToLocalTS` (lines 166-171; the changed line is now line 170).

**Root cause.** For years 0-99, `Date.UTC(y, ...)` interprets `y` as `19yy`, so luxon reverted it with `d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900)`. That only re-sets the *year* and keeps the month/day that JS had already normalized in the 19xx year. When the month/day overflowed (e.g. `plus({months:2, days:2})` from `0099-12-31` produces `{year:99, month:14, day:30}`, i.e. Feb 30 of year 100), the normalization used year **2000** (leap, 29-day Feb) and then the year was set to 100 (not leap, 28-day Feb), so the result was off by one day: `0100-03-01` instead of `0100-03-02`. Same class of error for year 0 (`0000-01-31 + 1 month` gave `0000-03-01` instead of `0000-02-29`, because 1900 is not a leap year but year 0 is).

**Diff:**
```diff
--- a/src/impl/util.js
+++ b/src/impl/util.js
@@ -166,7 +166,8 @@ export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
   // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
   if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
     d = new Date(d);
-    d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
+    // set the month and day again, this is necessary because year 2000 is a leap year, but year 100 is not
+    d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);
   }
   return +d;
 }
```
Setting year+month+day together makes JS normalize the overflow in the *correct* year (100 / 0), so leap-day clamping matches `daysInMonth`. No other source file was touched; the branch is only taken for `0 <= year < 100`, so years >= 100 (and negative years) are bit-for-bit unchanged.

