## Bug & fix

**Root cause:** `DateTime#isInDST` in `src/datetime.js` compared `this.offset` against `this.set({ month: 1 }).offset` to get the "standard time" reference. `set({ month: 1 })` keeps the day-of-month, so for any January DateTime the reference was *the same instant* (still in DST). In 1974 the US observed year-round DST (Jan 6 02:00 local → Oct 27 02:00 local), so for e.g. 1974-01-06 the Jan reference offset was the DST offset (-240 in NY) and `this.offset > janRef` was false, and the May reference was also -240, so `isInDST` returned false for the whole January–early-October 1974 DST period.

**Change (only file I edited): `src/datetime.js`, lines 1164–1173 (`get isInDST`)**

```diff
diff --git a/src/datetime.js b/src/datetime.js
index e909c0e..b052344 100644
--- a/src/datetime.js
+++ b/src/datetime.js
@@ -1166,7 +1166,8 @@ export default class DateTime {
       return false;
     } else {
       return (
-        this.offset > this.set({ month: 1 }).offset || this.offset > this.set({ month: 5 }).offset
+        this.offset > this.set({ month: 1, day: 1 }).offset ||
+        this.offset > this.set({ month: 5 }).offset
       );
     }
   }
```

`day: 1` pins the January reference to Jan 1 00:00 (true standard time in the northern hemisphere / non-DST in 1974 USA). This matches upstream Luxon's fix. `npx prettier --list-different src/datetime.js` → clean (exit 0). No other source file touched; I did not touch `src/impl/util.js` or `src/duration.js` (the other subagent's in-flight edits to those are present in the working tree but untouched by me). No test file added/edited/deleted in the repo.

